AB  - Climate‐smart cocoa practices are being championed as a strategy for addressing climate‐related risks in cocoa production systems. Despite growing recognition, adoption remains uneven due to structural, contextual, and knowledge‐related constraints. This study integrates evidence from two sources: a systematic review of 60 empirical studies and the first round of a modified Delphi study completed by 14 experts from four cocoa‐producing countries. Text mining of the recommendations extracted from these studies (124 statements consolidated into 60 study‐level documents) identified exploratory themes related to economic resilience, sustainable farming practices and capacity building, climate adaptation and extension services, and agroforestry. A complementary content analysis of the experts' open‐ended responses (54 responses received of a possible 154) revealed nuanced perspectives on the urgency of adoption, local understandings of climate change, implementation challenges, resistance to change, and the role of institutional networks. While both sources converged on the importance of strengthening extension systems and economic support, experts placed greater emphasis on locally grounded experiential knowledge, which was less visible in the literature. Lexical polarity of the literature‐derived recommendations was predominantly neutral or positive and did not differ by topic or by the country of the source study. The findings demonstrate the value of integrating computational and qualitative approaches to inform context‐sensitive and actionable climate‐smart cocoa policies.
